<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Hakam II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">915–976</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Mustansir bi-llah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hakam II was the second Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, reigning from 961 to 976 after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>. A scholar and bibliophile, he assembled a library said to hold some 400,000 volumes and enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a> with a richly decorated mihrab. His reign was peaceful and prosperous, but his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> fell under the control of the chamberlain al-Mansur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii.jpg" alt="al-Hakam II" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlHakemII_dirhem_MedinaAzahara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
